lly how she's so worried about her  son that she doesn't really care about  the possibility of her hand being  chopped off it's just such a  metaphorically a beautiful scene and one  thing that I want to note is how most of  these are on high levels and bones you  know is known for this a lot of like the  cameras are on eye level in in this  instance you know her crushing down on  the floor so we sort of look at her son  from below to up and that's sort of to  make the audiences real late and that's  why actually if you watch a lot of his  movie except in certain locations he  doesn't use extremely drastic shots like  from really below from really above it's  mostly just constantly  on the eye level and even when she's  like you know outside and tending to her  son everything is almost just I  allowable constantly to give that  emphasis on characters and people and  they the reason why his scenes are so  coherent and so good is because he's  known to be a good story boarder right  yeah he in fact doesn't shoot coverage  and he relies on his storyboards a lot  directors shooting coverage that means  they'll have a couple different camera  angles so that when they're editing they  have options to shoot from either like a  really close up or a little further away  but he draws everything out in  storyboards and usually and only films  that just what he needs just what he  needs so very rarely what will he shoot  any coverage because like coverage I  think some modern directors like kind of  overdo it yeah I'm I'm sort of thinking  about that scene from Thomas Crown  Affair where the two are playing chess  and there's just like inserts after  inserts and there's one overhead and all  sort of it I mean if used well I think  it's good but like it's very surprising  that he does that yeah it's definitely  I'm very unusual they made a book about  oak Joe another of bong Joon hos movies  and in the book there's a couple pages  that show some of the storyboards so I  just wanted to take a look at this one  it lays out this short sequence of  events storyboards also allow for some  technical notes to be thrown in along  with the just the image of what the  cameras gonna be capturing so for  example here it's a Steadicam and then a  lot of arrows as well to help convey  movement to help show kind of what the  cameras will be moving what the  characters are gonna be moving within  frame so in this storyboard this takes  place right after okiya has been taken  from her her giant pig that she's been  caring for for 10 years so let's take a  look at the scene here she figures out  that oak jaw has been taken right here  is he that's the first shot so if we  pause it's pretty similar to that first  storyboard Steadicam showing her running  following her as the arrows indicate  with the camera movement and right here  we get the cool sliding shot down the  hill that he again storyboarded and then  we're gonna get the close-up of her face  here  and then her feet moving with the  Steadicam movement showing she's slowing  down a little bit but still moving  forward  here's that windy road and as I said  it's a wide and then with the pan of the  camera the slow pan to the left and then  finally this last shot here in the  storyboard of her arriving over this  Overlook and seeing that the oak jaw in  the van is off in the distance oh yes he  had everything planned out we we often  use like the term visionary a lot when  when discussing good directors but I  think this is just it just shows how  visionary of a director he is so El  Camino came out this past weekend and we  just wanted to quickly highlight one  Easter egg that we thought was great  that maybe only superfans would have  caught so in this scene here Jesse's in  the diner and if you look at the floor  right here there's two Mis colored tiles  and they're in the shape of the Breaking  Bad title that is an amazing detail so  our team actually broke down a bunch of  other really interestin